 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach browsers over self-hosted WebSockets: the server pushes each new event revision to connected sessions, giving subsecond freshness under normal connectivity. Connection capacity, session management, and reconnect handling are operated by us rather than a realtime vendor.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together on this basis. The store publishes replayable revisions so delivery can fan them out live and browsers can catch up after a disconnect; delivery manages WebSocket sessions and reliable publication from the store; browsers subscribe over the socket and recover by replaying revisions from their last received one.
 
