 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** The server pushes committed revisions to browsers over WebSockets it operates itself. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected: it is the simplest stateless option, but every browser re-reads the board on an interval and updates lag by seconds. A hosted realtime service was rejected: it is the quickest route to low-latency updates, but brings recurring fees and couples the board to one provider. Self-hosted sockets get fast updates without a vendor dependency, at the cost of owning connection capacity and reconnect handling.
